<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SCE Forge conformance: the `floor(x)` builtin, and specifically what it does
  to NEGATIVE numbers.

  ⚠ WHY NEGATIVES ARE THE WHOLE POINT. `floor` moves toward −∞; truncation
  discards the fractional part and moves toward ZERO. They are identical on
  every non-negative input:

      floor(2.7) = 2      trunc(2.7) = 2      agree
      floor(-2.7) = -3    trunc(-2.7) = -2    DISAGREE

  A fixture of positives alone would pass against either lowering in all six
  backends, so the cases below are built around the negative side. `-0.5` is
  the sharpest of them: toward −∞ it is −1, toward zero it is 0, and an error
  that only shows up between −1 and 0 is exactly the kind a positives-only
  fixture ships.

  ⚠⚠ THIS FIXTURE ONCE HELD THE OPPOSITE ANSWERS, and that is why it exists.
  It was first written as `transform_truncation`, lowering to each backend's
  truncation, on the reasoning that the source notation's word means "discard
  the digits" and discarding goes toward zero. That was a reading of a WORD.
  The shipped implementation this generator has to agree with settles it the
  other way — it uses floor throughout and truncation nowhere, including at
  both of the places whose operand can actually go negative, where it writes
  `floor(x * 100.0) / 100.0`. A word's connotation is not evidence about a
  boundary; the code that has to match is.

  ⚠⚠⚠ A DIGIT COUNT NEEDS NO SECOND SIGNATURE. `floor(x * 100) / 100` is how
  that same implementation writes "round down to two decimals", and
  `floor(x / step) * step` is how it quantises to a grid. Both compose from
  this builtin plus arithmetic that already lowers, which is why `floor(x,
  digits)` is absent: it would be a second spelling of something the vocabulary
  can already say, and a second place for the boundary rule to drift.

  ⚠ The backends already agree here — C/C++ `std::floor`, Rust `f64::floor`,
  Go `math.Floor`, Python `math.floor`, Kotlin `kotlin.math.floor` all go
  toward −∞, so none needs the sign branch `round` needs in Python and Kotlin.
  That agreement is precisely what a later change could break without anything
  else noticing.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="transform" name="floor">
  <datamodel>
    <data id="value" sce:type="float64" sce:direction="in"/>
    <data id="floored" sce:type="int32" sce:direction="out" expr="floor(value)"/>
  </datamodel>
</scxml>
